 ### [Phong Shading](1853-phong-shading.md)
-Phong Shading is a local illumination model that approximates the direct lighting of a surface by summing ambient, diffuse, and specular components. It solves the problem of rendering smooth, shiny surfaces by interpolating surface normals across a polygon and evaluating the lighting equation per fragment, rather than per vertex. It differs from its siblings by using the angle between the view direction and the perfect reflection vector (or the half-vector in Blinn-Phong) to compute specular highlights, whereas [Blinn-Phong Shading Model](2239-blinn-phong-shading-model.md) uses the half-vector for computational efficiency, and [Phong Shading Model for Volume Data](1353-phong-shading-model-for-volume-data.md) adapts this to volumetric gradient-based shading.
+Phong Shading is a local illumination model that approximates direct lighting by summing ambient, diffuse, and specular components. It solves the problem of rendering smooth, shiny surfaces by evaluating the lighting equation per fragment. It differs from its siblings by computing specular highlights based on the angle between the view direction and the reflection vector, whereas [Blinn-Phong Shading Model](2239-blinn-phong-shading-model.md) uses the half-vector for computational efficiency, and [Phong Shading Model for Volume Data](1353-phong-shading-model-for-volume-data.md) adapts this to volumetric gradient-based shading.
 
